= Intrigues in the Imperial Court

Reports have arrived from the winter court at <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Axton**</span>. Empress Jacqueline Scilos, crowned three years ago and no longer treated by her ministers as a child in her father's chair, has addressed the assembled houses on the aims of her reign. She stated them as the reunification and pacification of the continent, to be pursued by every means available to her: diplomatic, dynastic, commercial, or military.

She did not name the Commonwealth. She did not need to. The Commonwealth was the Empire's second province within living memory, and the court has never accepted its loss.

Our diplomats are divided on how seriously to take the speech. Some see a young sovereign asserting herself before a court that still doubts her. Others point out that she used the word military in front of the officers who would have to carry it out, and that none of them objected.

The government and the press are asking for our position.

- @republican
- @concerning
- @imperial_origins

@republican
title: Reaffirm the party's republicanism.
subtitle: State that the Commonwealth's independence is not a matter for negotiation.
on-arrival: empire_posture -= 4 if empire_posture >= 4; center_dissent -= 3; left_dissent -= 3

We answer plainly. The Commonwealth was founded by its own people and will not be handed to a foreign court, whatever the Empress tells her houses. This is the oldest position in the party and the one our membership needs no persuading on. The centrists and the Left both rally to it, and dissent in both factions falls.

The Imperial legation notes the statement. Relations with the northern court cool slightly.

@concerning
title: Call the address concerning, and commit to nothing.
subtitle: Note it, and take no position.

We describe the address as a concerning development, urge the government to monitor the situation closely, and say nothing that could be quoted against us later. This is the safest response available, and it commits us to nothing.

@imperial_origins
title: Acknowledge the party's Imperial origins.
subtitle: Admit the shared history. Our own Left will not thank us for it.
on-arrival: empire_posture += 8 if empire_posture <= 92; center_dissent += 8; left_dissent += 8

We say the uncomfortable truth. The <span style="color: #c00000;">**Marchers**</span> were founded in the Empire, by Imperial workers in Imperial cities, decades before the Commonwealth existed. Our oldest lodges still hold charters issued in Axton. If the Empress speaks of a continent that should not be at war with itself, the idea is not foreign to us.

The Imperial court welcomes the statement. A reformist party in the former province that does not reject reunion outright is worth cultivating, and the Empire's posture toward us warms considerably.

Our own party does not welcome it. The centrists call it reckless and the Left calls it worse, and dissent in both factions rises sharply.
